(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cphims=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cphims.full||[]).push({
  num: 2, part: `Part One: The Inheritance`, title: `The Rulebook`, weeks: `Months 1 to 2`,
  tasks: [`E4`,`E2`,`E1`,`E3`,`C2`,`SE1`,`L9`,`L8`,`L10`],
  cast: [
    [`Joan Whitfield`,`Privacy officer, Cascade Valley Health. Precise, unflappable, allergic to surprises.`],
    [`Nate Ostrowski`,`CISO, Cascade Valley Health. Former Army signal officer.`],
    [`Bev Hart`,`Charge nurse, Greeley ICU. Precepted Maria on her first night shift, eighteen years ago.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `Maria was on Highway 34 by 7:10, driving toward the hospital where she'd spent fourteen years of nights.`,
    `Greeley looked the same from the parking lot. Brick, a newer glass wing, the helipad, the smokers' bench that had been moved three times and still drew smokers. What was different was the lobby. Four people in business clothes stood by the information desk with laptops and lanyards, and the hospital president was shaking their hands with the smile of a man who had been told about them twenty minutes ago.`,
    `The Joint Commission doesn't call ahead. That's the point. Surveyors show up, ask for the census, pick patients, and follow their care through the building to see whether the hospital does what its policies say. They talk to nurses. They open cabinets. They ask a tech on the night shift where the fire extinguisher is and watch where the tech's eyes go.`,
    `Her phone buzzed while she was still in the car. Marcus. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"laramie queue still clean. digging into why nobody got paged. don't have it yet."</span>`,
    `Then Kiran. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"When the backlog posted, the Laramie house supervisor texted the critical values to the nurses' cell phones. Just so you know before somebody else tells you."</span>`,
    `Then Ochoa, who was apparently already at his desk. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Greeley's yours for the week. Every tracer touches something you own. Don't hide anything and don't perform. Breakfast tomorrow, the diner, 6:30."</span>`,
    `Maria sat in the car a minute longer. Three hours of lab results that nobody saw. Four surveyors who would spend the week looking for exactly that kind of gap. She had owned all of it for about a month.`,
    `She clipped on her badge and went in through the employee entrance, the way she always had.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Ochoa's Legal Pad: who makes the rules`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Four kinds of rules`,
        body: [`The diner was loud at 6:30 in the morning. Ochoa didn't seem to notice. He wrote four words down the side of his legal pad: law, regulation, accreditation, local. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"Everything that tells you what you can't do with a patient's information comes from one of these. Know which one you're looking at."</span>`,
          `A law is passed by Congress or a state legislature. A regulation is the detailed rule a federal or state agency writes to carry out a law, and it has the force of law. Accreditation is voluntary, but most hospitals choose it: a private body like The Joint Commission surveys the hospital against its standards. Then there are state and local rules: licensing by the state health department, the state board of pharmacy, state privacy laws that can be stricter than federal ones, and state rules on how a health information exchange handles consent. HIPAA is a floor: a state law that protects privacy more than HIPAA isn't overridden by it.`,
          `The payers sit beside all of this. CMS runs Medicare and oversees Medicaid, which each state runs. CMS also writes the Conditions of Participation, the requirements a hospital has to meet to take Medicare and Medicaid patients at all. A hospital accredited by The Joint Commission can be deemed to meet them, which is why an unannounced survey makes a hospital president smile so hard.`],
        terms: [[`Conditions of Participation`,`The CMS requirements a hospital must meet to take part in Medicare and Medicaid, including patient rights and medical records.`],[`Deemed status`,`When an approved accreditor's survey is accepted as showing the hospital meets the Medicare Conditions of Participation.`],[`Tracer`,`A survey method where surveyors follow one patient's care through the hospital and talk to the staff who gave it.`]],
        exam: `Expect a question about who sets a requirement. Congress passes laws, agencies like CMS write regulations, accreditors like The Joint Commission survey against standards, and states license and add their own rules.` },
      { h: `HIPAA is two rules and a bigger stick`,
        body: [`The HIPAA Privacy Rule covers protected health information, or PHI, in any form: paper, spoken, electronic. It says when a covered entity may use or disclose it. Covered entities are health plans, clearinghouses, and providers who send standard transactions, like claims, electronically. Treatment, payment, and health care operations don't need the patient's authorization. Many other uses need a written authorization, though the rule also allows some disclosures without one, like those required by law or for public health. The minimum necessary standard says use only what the job needs, though it doesn't apply when one provider sends information to another for treatment. The rule also gives patients rights: to see and get a copy of their records, to ask for corrections, to get an accounting of certain disclosures, to ask for restrictions, and to get a notice of privacy practices.`,
          `The HIPAA Security Rule covers electronic PHI only. It requires a risk analysis and three kinds of safeguards: administrative, like policies and training; physical, like locked server rooms; and technical, like access controls, audit logs, and encryption. Its goals are confidentiality, integrity, and availability. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"People forget the third one,"</span> Ochoa said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"A chart nobody can open at three in the morning is a security failure too."</span>`,
          `HITECH made HIPAA bite. It added the breach notification rule, made business associates, the vendors who handle PHI for you, directly liable, and raised the penalties. It also paid hospitals to adopt certified EHRs. Every vendor that creates, receives, keeps, or sends PHI on your behalf signs a business associate agreement.`],
        terms: [[`PHI`,`Protected health information. Individually identifiable health information held by a covered entity or business associate.`],[`Minimum necessary`,`Use or disclose only the PHI needed for the purpose. It doesn't apply to disclosures to a provider for treatment.`],[`Business associate`,`A person or company that handles PHI on behalf of a covered entity, under a business associate agreement.`]],
        exam: `Know which rule is which. Privacy covers all PHI and patient rights. Security covers electronic PHI and its safeguards. HITECH added breach notification and direct liability for business associates.` },
      { h: `The rules that make data move`,
        body: [`For years the worry was data getting out. Now it's also data not getting where it should. The 21st Century Cures Act prohibits information blocking: a provider, a certified health IT developer, or a health information network or exchange may not interfere with access, exchange, or use of electronic health information unless a law requires it or an exception applies. The exceptions include preventing harm, privacy, security, and infeasibility. A blanket hold on test results so a doctor can call first isn't covered. A clinician's documented judgment that seeing it could endanger one specific patient's life or physical safety can be, and so can the patient's own request to wait.`,
          `Exchange runs on other rules too. ONC, now part of HHS's Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy office, certifies EHRs. TEFCA is a national framework that connects networks so records can follow patients across the country. Each HIE sets consent rules within state law, opt in or opt out. And 42 CFR Part 2 protects substance use disorder treatment records from federally assisted programs more tightly than HIPAA does. Recent changes let a patient sign one consent for treatment, payment, and operations, but the records still can't be used against the patient in a legal case without consent or a court order.`],
        terms: [[`Information blocking`,`A practice likely to interfere with access, exchange, or use of electronic health information, unless required by law or covered by an exception.`],[`42 CFR Part 2`,`Federal rules protecting the confidentiality of substance use disorder treatment records from federally assisted programs.`],[`TEFCA`,`The Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement, a national framework for exchange between health information networks.`]],
        exam: `If a question describes a delay or a barrier to sharing records, think information blocking first, then ask whether an exception fits the specific patient.` },
      { h: `Safety, rights, and the building`,
        body: [`EMTALA says a hospital with an emergency department that takes Medicare must give anyone who comes in a medical screening exam and stabilizing treatment, or an appropriate transfer, whether or not they can pay. Registration can't delay the screening to ask about insurance. Pharmacy has its own layer: state boards, pharmacist review of orders, and DEA rules that require two factor authentication to e-prescribe controlled substances. Environment of care covers the building itself: fire safety, utilities, and medical equipment. The Joint Commission now files these under Physical Environment. Emergency management standards sit beside them and cover what happens when the information systems go down. The FDA oversees software that works as a medical device.`,
          `<span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"And when the law's silent,"</span> Ochoa said, capping his pen, <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa">"you do the ethical thing and write down why. Somebody will ask."</span>`],
        terms: [[`EMTALA`,`The federal law requiring a medical screening exam and stabilizing care in the emergency department regardless of ability to pay.`],[`Environment of care`,`Accreditation standards for the physical setting: safety, security, fire, utilities, and medical equipment. The Joint Commission now files these under Physical Environment.`]],
        exam: `EMTALA questions usually hide inside a registration or downtime workflow. Any step that delays the screening exam to ask about payment is the wrong answer.` }
    ],
    video: [`7qBbsd2y4_Y`,`HIPAA at 30: Reflecting on Protecting Health Information`,`HHS Office for Civil Rights`,`12:46`]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f2s1`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E4`, title:`Her old unit`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The first tracer went to the ICU. Of course it did. Maria followed the surveyor, a nurse from Ohio with reading glasses pushed up into gray hair, through doors she could have opened in her sleep.`,`Bev Hart was charge nurse. Bev had precepted Maria on her first night shift eighteen years ago and still called her "kid." The surveyor asked Bev a simple question. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Your EHR goes down right now. How does a critical lab result reach you?"</span>`,`Bev opened her mouth and nothing came. Her eyes went to the shelf where the 2019 downtime binder used to be. It wasn't there. Then they went to Maria.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Step in and walk through the downtime process herself, so Bev doesn't look bad.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`Maria explains it clearly. The surveyor listens, writes something, and says, <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Thank you. I was asking the charge nurse."</span> Bev looks at the floor.`,
          why:`You know the answer, and you wanted to protect a friend. Surveyors aren't testing whether the VP knows the procedure. They're testing whether the nurse at 3 AM does. Answering for her hides the gap you need to see.`},
        {t:`Let Bev answer, then tell the surveyor plainly what's missing and what she owns.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Bev says she'd call the lab and the lab would call her. That's half right. Afterward Maria tells the surveyor that the unit's downtime binder is missing, that her team owns the downtime procedures, and that a recent outage at another hospital showed the same gap. The surveyor writes for a long time. Bev squeezes Maria's arm in the hallway. <span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Fond, wry, quietly proud.">"You always did tell on yourself, kid."</span>`,
          why:`Accreditation exists to find exactly this. Let staff answer for themselves, then be honest about the gap and who owns it. A surveyor who finds a problem you already named sees a hospital that learns. One who finds a problem you hid sees a hospital that performs.`},
        {t:`Tell the surveyor the downtime policy is being rewritten right now, so it's covered.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The surveyor asks to see the draft. There isn't one. The conversation gets noticeably cooler.`,
          why:`Spin is the worst answer in a survey. If a policy is in progress, show the work. If it isn't, don't say it is. Surveyors check, and once they catch one soft answer they look harder at everything.`},
        {t:`Suggest the surveyor trace a different patient, on the step down unit next door.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`The surveyor smiles politely and keeps her eyes on Bev. Redirecting a tracer is the kind of thing surveyors put in their notes.`,
          why:`The surveyor picks the patient and the path, not you. Steering a tracer away from a weak spot tells them where the weak spot is, and it isn't honest.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s2`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E3`, title:`Who owns the review`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At noon Maria took a call in a supply closet. The Laramie quality director wanted to know who was leading the review of last night. Three hours of delayed lab results, one critical potassium, no harm that anyone knew of yet.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"I can write it up,"</span> Marcus said on the same call. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"It's my interface."</span> Kiran offered to pull the list of every affected result. Everyone was helpful. Nobody was sure whose job it was.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have Marcus write the review, since he knows the interface better than anyone.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`Marcus writes four pages on queue settings. It's excellent. It doesn't mention the nurses who never got a call, the critical result policy, or why nobody on the floor noticed results had stopped.`,
          why:`The engineer owns the technical cause, not the safety review. A delayed critical result is a patient safety event. It needs the people who own clinical safety looking at the whole chain, not just the wire.`},
        {t:`Lead the review herself, since clinical systems owns the interface and the outage.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`She starts it. By Wednesday she's interviewing nurses about callback practice, which isn't her job, and the quality director is quietly annoyed that IT is running a safety review.`,
          why:`Ownership instinct is good. But you're a participant in this event, not the neutral owner of the review. Patient safety leads it. You bring the systems facts and fix what's yours.`},
        {t:`File a safety event report and let patient safety lead, with IT on the team.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`She files the report from the closet. The patient safety officer takes the lead by two o'clock and names a team: the Laramie lab, nursing, the house supervisor, Marcus for the technical cause, and Maria for the systems side. The review asks two questions: why it broke and why nobody knew. Marcus gets the first one.`,
          why:`Know who owns what. Patient safety and quality own the event review. IT owns the technical root cause and the fix. Clinical leaders own the downtime and callback practice. Reporting it through the event system is how the organization learns instead of just one department.`},
        {t:`Wait until the survey is over before opening a formal review of the Laramie event.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Nine days later the details are fuzzy. The house supervisor has rotated to days, and the lab tech who noticed something odd at 10 PM doesn't remember what.`,
          why:`Events get reviewed while the facts are fresh, survey or no survey. Delaying a safety review to keep it out of sight is the opposite of what accreditation is looking for.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s3`, domain:`Systems Management`, task:`SE1`, title:`The texts`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Laramie house supervisor called back that afternoon. She sounded like she'd been up all night, because she had.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman" data-tone="Defiant, frayed, unapologetic.">"I texted the criticals to the nurses' personal phones. Name, room, value. I know. I'd do it again. It was faster than walking six units."</span>`,`She wasn't wrong that it was faster. She also wasn't wrong that it put patient names on a dozen personal phones. Cascade's policy didn't say anything about downtime messaging at all.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write a policy that bans PHI in any text message, with no exceptions at all.`, s:1, d:{trust:-2,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`It's clear and easy to enforce. The next time results stop flowing, nurses will walk six units again, or quietly text anyway.`,
          why:`Protecting confidentiality is right. But a policy that ignores availability gets broken in the next emergency. Good policy protects both, and gives people a safe way to do the urgent thing.`},
        {t:`Thank her, and let downtime texting stand as long as the phones are locked.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-2,health:-3},
          after:`The habit spreads to two more hospitals by spring. A phone with eleven patients' lab values is left in a rideshare in Cheyenne.`,
          why:`Personal phones are outside every control you have. You can't audit them, wipe them, or know who else reads them. Kindness to one tired supervisor isn't a policy.`},
        {t:`Write a downtime messaging policy with privacy and security, naming one secure channel.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Joan Whitfield, the privacy officer, and Nate Ostrowski, the CISO, meet with the house supervisor Thursday. The draft names the hospital's secure messaging app on unit phones as the only channel for PHI, keeps the phone callback for critical results, and spells out who verifies the values once the system is back. The house supervisor reads it and says, <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Okay. That I can do at 2 AM."</span>`,
          why:`Security policy has three goals: confidentiality, integrity, and availability. A good downtime policy keeps urgent information moving, keeps it off devices you can't control, and makes sure what was sent gets checked against the record later.`},
        {t:`Report the supervisor to HR for a violation, so other sites take it seriously.`, s:0, d:{trust:-6,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`There was no policy to violate. Word gets around Wyoming in a day: Fort Collins punished the person who got the potassium to the nurse.`,
          why:`You can't discipline someone for breaking a rule that didn't exist, especially when she was protecting patients. Fix the policy gap. Then train to it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s4`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L10`, title:`Fresh stickers`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 8 PM the Greeley nurse manager for the medical floors caught Maria in the parking garage. Doug had a box of new downtime binders under one arm and a sheet of green stickers in his hand.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Printed twenty of these. I'll put one on every unit tonight. The stickers say reviewed. I figured I'd date them last spring, so it doesn't look like we scrambled."</span>`,`He looked exhausted and proud of himself. He'd had a long day too.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Thank him and let it go. The binders are real, and the dates are a small thing.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`On day three a surveyor asks a night nurse when she last reviewed the binder. The nurse says she's never seen it before tonight. The sticker says April.`,
          why:`A backdated review is a false record. It's small until someone checks, and then it's the only thing anyone remembers about your survey. Ethics doesn't have a size threshold.`},
        {t:`Put the binders out tonight, but with today's date and no reviewed stickers.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:5,health:3},
          after:`Doug winces, then laughs. <span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Sheepish, laughing at himself.">"Yeah. Okay. That was dumb."</span> They put the binders out together, dated today. When a surveyor asks the next morning, the nurse says they came out last night, and she read hers. The surveyor nods.`,
          why:`Fixing a gap fast is good. Faking when you fixed it isn't. Put the real fix in place, date it truthfully, and let the record show what actually happened. Honesty with an accreditor is an ethical duty, and it's also the better strategy.`},
        {t:`Hold the binders back until the survey is over, so nothing looks rushed.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`The units go another three days without downtime binders, survey or not. If anything goes down this week, nurses will be guessing.`,
          why:`You avoided the false record. But you also left a known safety gap open to protect appearances. The binder helps patients tonight. Put it out honestly.`},
        {t:`Tell him to ask the CNO, since unit documents are a nursing decision, not hers.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The CNO's phone goes to voicemail. Doug decides that silence means yes.`,
          why:`The documents are nursing's, but the downtime procedures are yours, and you were standing right there. When you see an ethics problem in front of you, you don't hand it off and walk to your car.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s5`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E4`, title:`The front desk`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Day two, the emergency department. The surveyor asked to see how the ED registers patients when Corvane is down. A registrar pulled the paper downtime form and walked through it with a made-up patient, a teenager with a broken wrist.`,`The paper form had been copied years ago from a registration screen two systems back. Insurance carrier and policy number were the first two boxes. The triage section was on the back.`,`The registrar did exactly what the form said and asked the pretend teenager about insurance first. The surveyor wrote it down.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Explain to the surveyor that the paper form is rarely used, since downtime is rare.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The surveyor asks why a form nobody uses is in the downtime kit. It's a fair question.`,
          why:`If it's in the kit, someone will use it at 3 AM. And EMTALA doesn't care whether the delay was caused by a screen, a form, or a habit. Fix the workflow.`},
        {t:`Redesign the form so triage comes first and payment questions come after screening.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`She has a registration analyst pull the electronic quick registration build, and it has the same order. Both get fixed within the week with the ED director and compliance at the table: name, birth date, complaint, triage. Insurance after the medical screening exam starts.`,
          why:`EMTALA requires a medical screening exam and stabilizing care regardless of ability to pay. Registration can go on in parallel, but asking about insurance can't delay the screening. Check both the paper downtime form and the electronic build, since they drift apart.`},
        {t:`Retrain the registrars to skip the insurance boxes until the nurse sees the patient.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`The training helps. But the form still puts insurance first, and every new registrar learns the form before they learn the exception.`,
          why:`Training is part of it. When a form or a screen drives the wrong behavior, though, fix the form. Don't ask people to remember to ignore it.`},
        {t:`Move insurance to the back of the paper form, and leave the electronic build alone.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`The paper is fixed. Corvane's quick registration screen, which nobody checked, has the same order, and it's what the ED uses every day.`,
          why:`Downtime forms copy screens, and screens drift. When you find a compliance problem in one, check the other. The everyday workflow matters more than the backup.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s6`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, task:`C2`, title:`Not quite de-identified`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Aisha Rahman found Maria in the cafeteria at lunch, between tracers. Aisha was the CMIO, a hospitalist who moved fast and thought in data.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Quick favor. My fellow needs a de-identified extract for a sepsis study. All septic admissions, last two years, all eleven hospitals. No names, no MRNs. Just admit date, discharge date, ZIP code, age, labs, outcome. Can Kiran run it this week?"</span>`,`Maria looked at the list on the napkin. Admit dates. ZIP codes.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Run it as written, since no names or record numbers means it's de-identified.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The extract goes out. A month later Joan reads the study protocol and calls Maria. Exact dates and five digit ZIP codes in a rural county can point straight at a person.`,
          why:`Removing names isn't de-identification. Under the HIPAA Safe Harbor method, dates more specific than the year and most ZIP code detail have to go too. With them in, it's still PHI.`},
        {t:`Call it what it is, a limited data set, and route it to Joan for an agreement.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Aisha frowns, then nods. <span class="said" data-who="Aisha">"Fine. You're right, the dates are the whole study."</span> Maria sends it to Joan Whitfield, the privacy officer, who sets up a data use agreement and checks the research approval. The fellow gets the extract in two weeks, legally.`,
          why:`Words matter. Data with dates and ZIP codes but no direct identifiers is a limited data set. It can be used for research under a data use agreement. Truly de-identified data has those removed too. Know the terms, and route each to the right owner.`},
        {t:`Strip the dates and ZIP codes so it's fully de-identified, then run it today.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:2,health:1},
          after:`It's clean and legal. It's also useless. Aisha's fellow can't measure time to antibiotics without dates.`,
          why:`You protected privacy, which is right. But you decided what the study needs without asking. A limited data set under an agreement would have kept the dates legally.`},
        {t:`Tell Aisha research data isn't a clinical systems job, and send it to the warehouse.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`The warehouse team runs exactly what Aisha wrote. Nobody asked the privacy question, because everyone assumed someone else had.`,
          why:`Handing off the work doesn't hand off the problem. You saw that the request was mislabeled. Say so, and get it to the right person.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s7`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E2`, title:`The consent question`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Marcus called that evening with what sounded like good news. The regional HIE had noticed Laramie's feed went quiet during the outage, and while they were at it, they'd asked a question.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Marcus">"They want to move Cheyenne, Laramie, and Rawlins under Cascade's HIE agreement. Right now those three still send through the old MedaLink arrangement. I can repoint the feeds Friday. One problem. Rawlins has the behavioral health clinic, and its substance use treatment program. And MedaLink kept its own opt out list. I don't know what Cascade's agreement does with either."</span>`,`It was a technical question on the surface. Maria knew it wasn't.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Repoint the feeds Friday. Better continuity of care is worth a few loose ends.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-4,health:-5},
          after:`The feeds move. Three hundred patients who opted out under MedaLink are now sharing records through the exchange, and nobody told them.`,
          why:`Continuity of care matters, but not more than a patient's documented choice. When consent rules change, you sort them out before the data moves. Undoing a disclosure is impossible.`},
        {t:`Hold the Rawlins clinic back, move the rest, and brief Joan once it's done.`, s:1, flag:`skippedprivacy`, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:-1},
          after:`It looks careful. The clinic stays out. But the old opt out list doesn't carry over to Cascade's agreement, and Joan hears about the change from the HIE's liaison the next morning, not from Maria.`,
          why:`You spotted the substance use records, which is good. Then you made a privacy decision without the privacy officer. Consent rules, state law, and 42 CFR Part 2 are Joan's call, and Joan should hear it from you first.`},
        {t:`Bring Joan in first to map every consent rule before any of the feeds move.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:5},
          after:`Maria calls Joan Whitfield that night. Joan answers on the second ring and asks three questions Maria hadn't thought of. The plan they agree on carries the MedaLink opt out list into the new agreement, keeps the clinic's Part 2 records out unless a patient consents, and moves the feeds two weeks later than Marcus wanted.`,
          why:`HIE consent depends on state law, the exchange's rules, the patient's recorded choice, and for substance use treatment, 42 CFR Part 2. That's a privacy decision. Bring the privacy officer in before you build, not after.`},
        {t:`Turn off the Wyoming feeds to the HIE until One Chart is live and settles it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-4},
          after:`For a year, an ED in Fort Collins can't see a Rawlins patient's medication list. Continuity of care was the reason the HIE existed.`,
          why:`Stopping exchange to avoid a hard question just moves the harm to the next caregiver. And cutting off a feed without a good reason can look a lot like information blocking.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s8`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E4`, title:`Seventy-two hours`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Day three. The Greeley medical staff president caught Maria after a meeting with a printed request signed by twenty-two physicians.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Patients see their lab results in the portal before we do. They panic and call at night. We want a seventy-two hour hold on every result before it releases. Your team can build that, right?"</span>`,`Her team could build it in an afternoon. That was the problem.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Build the hold for all results, since the physicians have a real patient concern.`, s:0, d:{trust:3,conf:-4,health:-3},
          after:`The hold goes live. Within a month a patient complains to the federal government that she couldn't see her own biopsy result for three days.`,
          why:`A blanket delay so clinicians can review first is very likely information blocking under the Cures Act. The physicians' concern is real. The fix can't be a hold on every patient's records.`},
        {t:`Hold only abnormal results, and release normal ones right away as they post.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`It feels like a compromise. Compliance reads it and points out that abnormal results are exactly the ones patients most need to see, and it's still a blanket delay.`,
          why:`Narrowing a blanket rule doesn't make it an exception. The preventing harm exception needs a judgment about one specific patient, not a category of results.`},
        {t:`Release on time, and let a clinician hold one result to protect a patient's safety.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:5,health:4},
          after:`Maria brings compliance and Joan into the room. Results release on time unless a law says otherwise. A physician can hold one specific patient's result after documenting that seeing it could endanger that patient's life or physical safety. Worry about a result isn't enough. Patients can choose to wait for a call. Kiran adds a portal note that says the doctor will follow up. The medical staff president isn't thrilled, but he signs it.`,
          why:`Information blocking rules expect results to reach patients without unnecessary delay. Exceptions exist, like preventing harm for an individual patient or a patient's own request to wait, but a blanket hold isn't one. Give clinicians tools to follow up with patients.`},
        {t:`Turn off the portal's results release for now, until legal reviews the request.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Every patient across eleven hospitals loses access to results for three weeks while legal reviews it.`,
          why:`Turning off access to study a delay is a bigger delay. Keep the current lawful practice running while you decide.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s9`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L9`, title:`The spreadsheet`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`That afternoon her phone buzzed with a text.`,
        ((s.flags.skippedprivacy||G.skippedprivacy)
          ?`<prop type="text" from="Joan Whitfield" time="3:12 PM">This is Joan Whitfield, privacy. I keep hearing about your projects from other people. Anything from Laramie I should know about?</prop>`
          :`<prop type="text" from="Joan Whitfield" time="3:12 PM">Joan Whitfield, privacy. I hear Laramie had a rough night. Anything from it I should know about?</prop>`),
        `The spreadsheet was Kiran's list of every delayed Laramie result, with names, record numbers, and values. A Laramie lab analyst had emailed it to a personal account to work on at home. The analyst had told Kiran, and Kiran had told Maria, and Maria hadn't told anyone yet.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Have the analyst delete the email and the file, then remind the lab about policy.`, s:0, d:{trust:1,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`The analyst deletes it. Nobody can say whether the account forwarded it, synced it, or backed it up. And Joan finds out it happened from the lab, a week later.`,
          why:`Fixing a privacy event quietly is hiding it. The privacy officer owns the breach risk assessment. You don't get to decide alone that something wasn't a breach.`},
        {t:`Call Joan back now, share every fact, and let Joan run the breach assessment.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:5,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`Joan listens without interrupting, then asks for the account type, the time, and whether the analyst forwarded it. The assessment takes two days. Joan handles the analyst with care. When it's done, Joan says, <span class="said" data-who="Joan" data-tone="Dry, approving, understated.">"You called me before I had to call you. Keep doing that."</span>`,
          why:`Report privacy events to the person who owns them, quickly and completely. The privacy officer decides, through a documented risk assessment, whether it's a reportable breach. Your job is facts and cooperation, and fixing whatever process let it happen.`},
        {t:`Wait until the survey ends Friday, then bring the whole thing to Joan in one meeting.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`Joan's face, on Friday, is very still. Breach rules run on a clock that starts when the organization knows, and the organization has known since Wednesday.`,
          why:`The clock for breach assessment starts at discovery, not when it's convenient. Never hold a privacy event to get past a survey.`},
        {t:`Ask Nate's team to check the account for forwarding before she brings it to Joan.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:2,health:0},
          after:`Nate's analyst finds no forwarding rule. It's useful. It also took a day, and Joan would have asked for it anyway, with the right questions.`,
          why:`Security can help with the facts, and often should. But the privacy officer owns the assessment and directs the investigation. Bring them in first.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s10`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E1`, title:`Not a hospital`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By Thursday Maria had a draft plan to fix downtime procedures across Cascade. One binder template, one set of steps, one drill schedule, for every site.`,`Kiran read it and tapped one line. <span class="said" data-who="Kiran">"Home health nurses are in people's living rooms. They don't have a unit, or a binder shelf, or a lab down the hall. And the clinics close at five."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Keep one template for everyone, and let each site adjust it locally as needed.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Each site adjusts it differently. Within a year there are forty versions and no one knows which is current.`,
          why:`A common standard is good. But leaving every site to adapt it alone means the hard cases, like home health, get the least help.`},
        {t:`Build procedures for each kind of site: hospital, critical access, clinic, and home.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Home health gets offline visit notes on a tablet and a call tree. The clinics get a schedule printout at close of day. Rawlins, a critical access hospital with one IT tech, gets a simpler version Cheyenne will back up. It's four plans that share one spine.`,
          why:`Different kinds of organizations deliver different services in different places. A home health agency, a clinic, a critical access hospital, and a referral center each need downtime procedures that fit how they actually work.`},
        {t:`Leave home health and the clinics out, since the survey only covers the hospital.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`In March a network outage hits the home health agency. Nurses at a dozen houses can't see medication lists. Nobody wrote anything down for them.`,
          why:`Surveys don't define your obligations. Patients in their homes and clinics are your patients too, and their care stops when systems stop.`},
        {t:`Ask the vendors for their standard downtime kit and give that to every site.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Corvane's kit is generic and assumes a hospital unit. MedaLink's is from 2014.`,
          why:`Vendor templates are a starting point. They don't know your sites, your services, or your patients.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s11`, domain:`Leadership`, task:`L8`, title:`Policy by email`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Thursday night Maria drafted an email to every clinical manager: from now on, all downtime procedures follow the attached steps. It felt good to write. She had her finger over Send.`,`Then she thought about the 2019 binder nobody could find, and the fact that nobody could tell her who had approved it.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Send the email tonight. Speed matters, and the formal policy can catch up later.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The email goes out. It's in four hundred inboxes and no policy library. In a year nobody will know if it's still the rule.`,
          why:`An email isn't a policy. It has no owner, no review date, and no home. That's how the 2019 binder ended up as a mystery.`},
        {t:`Take it through policy review with an owner, approval, version, and review date.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:5,health:3},
          after:`She files it with the policy committee. The procedure gets a named owner, an approval path through nursing and IT, a version number, a review date a year out, and a training plan. The old 2019 version is formally retired so nobody finds it again.`,
          why:`Policies and procedures need governance: an owner, an approval path, version control, a place everyone can find them, training, and a review cycle. That's what makes them real at 3 AM and defensible in a survey.`},
        {t:`Post it on the intranet as a guideline until the policy committee meets next month.`, s:1, d:{trust:1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`It's findable, which is better. But a guideline isn't binding, and two managers ignore it because it isn't official.`,
          why:`Putting it somewhere visible helps. Calling it a guideline tells people it's optional. Route it through the policy process and say so.`},
        {t:`Ask Ochoa to sign it as the CIO, so managers know it carries his authority.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:0},
          after:`Ochoa reads it and asks who in nursing approved it. Nobody did. He doesn't sign.`,
          why:`A signature from the top isn't the same as the right approval. Clinical procedures need clinical owners in the approval path.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f2s12`, domain:`Environments`, task:`E4`, title:`The daughter`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Friday morning, the last day of the survey, a woman stopped Maria at the ICU desk. Her mother had been discharged to a rehab center in Loveland, and the rehab doctor wanted the records.`,`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"I'm her health care power of attorney. I have the paper. Medical records told me they can only print it, it's twenty-five cents a page, and it's eleven hundred pages. Can't you just put it on a flash drive?"</span>`,`She looked about forty. She had the same tired eyes Maria saw in the mirror after night shift. For a second Maria thought of Lucia, someday, standing at a desk like this one.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Give her the records now, since she's clearly family and it's for her mother's care.`, s:0, d:{trust:2,conf:-3,health:-2},
          after:`It feels kind. Nobody checks the power of attorney. If this daughter had been the wrong daughter, the hospital would never have known.`,
          why:`Being family isn't the same as having authority. Under HIPAA a personal representative has the right of access, but you verify the authority before you hand anything over.`},
        {t:`Verify her authority, then give an electronic copy at a reasonable cost-based fee.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`Maria walks her to medical records, checks the power of attorney with the HIM manager, and asks why it has to be paper. There's no good reason. The daughter leaves with an encrypted electronic copy that afternoon. The HIM manager adds a note to fix the default.`,
          why:`Patients and their personal representatives have a right to access their records, in the electronic form they ask for if it's readily producible, for a reasonable cost-based fee. Verify identity and authority first. Paper only is a barrier, not a rule.`},
        {t:`Tell her HIPAA means only the patient herself can request copies of the records.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-2,health:-2},
          after:`The daughter leaves in tears. The rehab doctor waits four more days.`,
          why:`That's a common myth. A personal representative with legal authority stands in the patient's shoes for access. Saying no blocks care and the patient's own rights.`},
        {t:`Send the records directly to the rehab doctor, since that's a treatment disclosure.`, s:1, d:{trust:2,conf:1,health:2},
          after:`The rehab doctor gets what's needed by noon. The daughter still has no copy, and she asked for one.`,
          why:`Sending to another provider for treatment is allowed and it helps the patient. But the daughter made an access request, and she's entitled to her own copy too.`}
      ]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f2s8`, title:`Which rule is it?`, intro:`New health IT leaders mix up which law, rule, or body governs a situation. Pick the one that fits.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`An ED registrar must not delay a patient's medical screening exam to ask about insurance.`, choices:[`HIPAA Privacy Rule`,`EMTALA`,`HITECH`,`The Joint Commission`], answer:1, why:`EMTALA requires a medical screening exam and stabilizing care regardless of ability to pay, and payment questions can't delay it.`},
      {prompt:`A hospital must have audit logs, access controls, and a risk analysis for its electronic patient data.`, choices:[`HIPAA Security Rule`,`42 CFR Part 2`,`Conditions of Participation`,`EMTALA`], answer:0, why:`The Security Rule covers electronic PHI and requires administrative, physical, and technical safeguards, including a risk analysis.`},
      {prompt:`A clinic that treats substance use disorder with federal funding needs patient consent before sharing records through the HIE.`, choices:[`HITECH`,`EMTALA`,`42 CFR Part 2`,`ONC certification`], answer:2, why:`42 CFR Part 2 protects substance use disorder treatment records from federally assisted programs more tightly than HIPAA.`},
      {prompt:`A health system builds a rule that holds every lab result from the portal for three days.`, choices:[`HIPAA Security Rule`,`The Joint Commission`,`FDA device rules`,`Information blocking rules`], answer:3, why:`The 21st Century Cures Act information blocking provisions prohibit unnecessary interference with patients' access to their electronic health information.`},
      {prompt:`The requirements a hospital must meet to take Medicare and Medicaid patients.`, choices:[`Conditions of Participation`,`TEFCA`,`The HIPAA Privacy Rule`,`The state pharmacy board`], answer:0, why:`CMS sets the Conditions of Participation. Accreditation from an approved body can deem a hospital to meet them.`},
      {prompt:`A vendor that stores PHI for the hospital becomes directly liable for protecting it, and breaches must be reported.`, choices:[`EMTALA`,`HITECH`,`TEFCA`,`The Joint Commission`], answer:1, why:`HITECH extended direct liability to business associates and added the breach notification rule.`},
      {prompt:`A patient asks for a list of certain people and organizations her records were disclosed to.`, choices:[`EMTALA`,`Information blocking rules`,`HIPAA Privacy Rule`,`42 CFR Part 2`], answer:2, why:`The Privacy Rule gives patients the right to an accounting of certain disclosures of their PHI.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which federal law requires a hospital emergency department to provide a medical screening exam regardless of the patient's ability to pay?`, opts:[`HIPAA`,`EMTALA`,`HITECH`,`The Cures Act`], a:1, why:`EMTALA requires Medicare-participating hospitals with emergency departments to screen and stabilize anyone who comes in, regardless of ability to pay.`, task:`E4`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`Physicians ask IT to hold every lab result from the patient portal for 72 hours so they can review results first. What is the BEST response?`, opts:[`Build the hold, since physicians own result release`,`Hold only abnormal results and release normal ones`,`Release on time, with holds for individual patients`,`Turn off portal results until legal reviews it`], a:2, why:`A blanket delay for clinician review is likely information blocking. The preventing harm exception can cover an individual determination that access would endanger a specific patient's life or physical safety, and patients may ask to wait.`, task:`E4`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Why does accreditation by The Joint Commission matter to a hospital's Medicare participation?`, opts:[`It replaces the hospital's state license`,`It can deem the hospital to meet CMS rules`,`It exempts the hospital from HIPAA audits`,`It certifies the hospital's EHR software`], a:1, why:`An approved accreditor's survey can deem a hospital to meet the CMS Conditions of Participation, which are required to take Medicare and Medicaid patients.`, task:`E4`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A patient's daughter, who holds the patient's health care power of attorney, asks for an electronic copy of the record. What should the hospital do?`, opts:[`Refuse, since only the patient may request it`,`Give it at once, since she is immediate family`,`Print it, since paper is the standard format`,`Verify authority, then give an electronic copy`], a:3, why:`A personal representative has the patient's right of access. Verify authority, then provide the record in the electronic form requested if readily producible, for a reasonable cost-based fee.`, task:`E4`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A hospital plans to send records from three newly joined sites through the regional HIE under a different agreement. One site runs a federally assisted substance use clinic. What should happen FIRST?`, opts:[`Move the feeds and correct any issues later`,`Have the privacy officer map the consent rules`,`Stop all exchange from the three sites for now`,`Exclude the clinic and move the remaining feeds`], a:1, why:`HIE consent depends on state law, the exchange's rules, patients' recorded choices, and 42 CFR Part 2. That's a privacy decision to settle before data moves.`, task:`E2`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`Which organization type typically provides primary care in underserved areas and serves patients regardless of their ability to pay?`, opts:[`Critical access hospital`,`Ambulatory surgery center`,`Community health center`,`Long term acute care hospital`], a:2, why:`Community health centers provide primary care in underserved areas and serve patients regardless of ability to pay, often on a sliding fee scale.`, task:`E1`, domain:`Environments`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`An interface outage delays critical lab results to a hospital for three hours. Who should lead the patient safety event review?`, opts:[`The patient safety officer, with IT on the team`,`The interface engineer who restored the feed`,`The VP of clinical systems who owns the interface`,`The CIO, after the next accreditation survey`], a:0, why:`Patient safety and quality own event reviews. IT owns the technical root cause and supports the review; it shouldn't lead a review of its own event.`, task:`E3`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A research extract contains admission dates, five digit ZIP codes, ages, and lab values, with no names or record numbers. How should it be classified?`, opts:[`De-identified data`,`A limited data set`,`Aggregate statistics`,`Public use data`], a:1, why:`A limited data set excludes direct identifiers but may keep dates and ZIP codes. It's still PHI and needs a data use agreement. De-identified data removes those too.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Under HIPAA, which disclosure is NOT subject to the minimum necessary standard?`, opts:[`To a provider for treatment`,`To a health plan for payment`,`To a billing company vendor`,`To an internal quality audit`], a:0, why:`Minimum necessary doesn't apply to disclosures to a provider for treatment, to the patient, or under the patient's authorization. Payment and operations uses still follow it.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`During an outage, a supervisor texts critical lab values to nurses' personal phones. Which policy response BEST protects confidentiality and availability?`, opts:[`Ban any PHI in text messages, with no exceptions`,`Allow the texting as long as phones are locked`,`Name one secure channel and keep phone callbacks`,`Refer the supervisor to HR for a policy violation`], a:2, why:`Good security policy balances confidentiality, integrity, and availability. An approved secure channel keeps urgent information moving and off devices the organization can't control.`, task:`SE1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A downtime plan that keeps lab results reaching caregivers when the EHR is unavailable mainly supports which security goal?`, opts:[`Integrity`,`Availability`,`Confidentiality`,`Non-repudiation`], a:1, why:`Availability means authorized users can get the information when they need it. Downtime procedures protect availability.`, task:`SE1`, domain:`Systems Management`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`An analyst emails a spreadsheet of patient names and lab values to a personal account. What should the clinical systems leader do FIRST?`, opts:[`Have the analyst delete the file and the email`,`Ask security to check the account for forwarding`,`Wait until the survey ends, then review it`,`Report it to the privacy officer right away`], a:3, why:`The privacy officer owns the breach risk assessment and directs the investigation. Report quickly and completely; don't decide alone that it wasn't a breach.`, task:`L9`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`A leader writes a new downtime procedure. What MOST makes it an enforceable organizational policy?`, opts:[`An email sent to every clinical manager`,`A signature from the CIO's own office`,`A guideline posted on the intranet page`,`An owner, approval path, review date`], a:3, why:`Policies need governance: an owner, the right approval path, version control, a findable home, training, and a review cycle.`, task:`L8`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`A unit's local downtime binder tells nurses to text critical lab values to the charge nurse. The system's downtime policy forbids it, and the unit says its way is faster. What should the leader do?`, opts:[`Let the unit keep it, since it has worked so far`,`Send the unit an email correcting the one step`,`Pull it, then revise it with the policy owner`,`Rewrite the system policy to match the unit`], a:2, why:`The approved policy governs. A local procedure that conflicts with it comes down, and any good idea in it goes through the policy owner's approval path, then into training. An email leaves the old binder on the shelf, and rewriting the policy to match one unit would put PHI back on phones nobody controls.`, task:`L8`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AN`},
    {q:`After an accreditation finding, a leader must tell 300 staff about new downtime procedures. Which message is MOST effective?`, opts:[`The finding, what changes, and how to ask`,`A copy of the final survey report alone`,`A reminder that the policy is mandatory`,`A note that the surveyors were too strict`], a:0, why:`Good business communication says what happened, what changes for the reader, and how to ask questions. A report, a threat, or blame doesn't help anyone act.`, task:`L12`, domain:`Leadership`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Under HIPAA, health plans, clearinghouses, and providers that send standard transactions like claims electronically are called:`, opts:[`Business associates`,`Hybrid entities`,`Data stewards`,`Covered entities`], a:3, why:`Covered entities are the health plans, clearinghouses, and providers who bill electronically. Business associates are the vendors who handle PHI on a covered entity's behalf and sign an agreement to protect it. A hybrid entity is one organization that runs both covered and non-covered business.`, task:`C2`, domain:`Clinical Informatics`, level:`RE`},
    {q:`A health system is writing one downtime procedure for all its sites. Which site is MOST likely to need its own version because of how it delivers care?`, opts:[`A home health agency whose nurses visit homes`,`The medical unit at the main referral hospital`,`The intensive care unit at the main hospital`,`The emergency department at the main campus`], a:0, why:`Organization types deliver care differently. Home health nurses work in patients' homes with no unit, binder shelf, or lab down the hall, so they need offline visit notes and a call tree. Units inside one hospital share the same building, lab, and paper backup, so one hospital procedure can serve them.`, task:`E1`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AP`},
    {q:`Two months after a hospital replaced its lab system, the state health department calls. It hasn't received reportable disease results since go-live, and the project's interface list has no entry for the state. What does this MOST likely show?`, opts:[`The plan missed an outside data partner`,`The state changed its reporting format`,`The lab stopped running reportable tests`,`The new lab software has a coding defect`], a:0, why:`A system change touches every organization connected by data, and public health is one of the easiest to forget. The timing points at the project, because the feed stopped at go-live. A format change or a defect gets checked during the fix, but the root cause is an inventory of stakeholders and interfaces that stopped at the hospital's walls.`, task:`E2`, domain:`Environments`, level:`AN`},
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `The exit conference was at three o'clock on Friday in the Greeley board room. Four surveyors at one end of the table, the hospital's leaders at the other, Ochoa in a chair along the wall like furniture, which was what he'd promised.`,
    `The lead surveyor read the summary in a flat, kind voice. Medication management, no findings. Infection control, no findings. Patient rights, no findings. Then she turned a page.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Preliminary finding: downtime procedures">The hospital does not consistently ensure that staff can carry out downtime procedures for its information systems. Downtime materials were missing or outdated on two units, and staff interviewed could not describe how critical results would reach them during an outage.</prop>`,
    `One finding. The hospital would stay accredited. And the one thing the surveyors had written up was the thing Maria owned.`,
    ((s.flags.skippedprivacy||G.skippedprivacy)
      ?`Joan Whitfield sat across the table and didn't look at her. Maria still owed Joan a conversation about the HIE, and they both knew it.`
      :`Joan Whitfield, across the table, caught her eye and gave a small nod. It might have meant sorry. It might have meant good. With Joan, she was learning, it could mean both.`),
    `In the parking lot Ochoa walked her to her car. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, approving, firm.">"You told them before they found it,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Ochoa" data-tone="Quiet, approving, firm.">"They noticed. Now go find out why your hospitals didn't know their own procedures. Then fix that, not the binders."</span>`,
    `Lucia was at the kitchen table when she got home, laptop open, a nursing school essay prompt on the screen. Describe a time a rule protected a patient. She'd written two sentences and deleted them.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"Do you have one?"</span> she asked. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia">"A rule that actually saved somebody?"</span>`,
    `Maria thought about Bev's empty shelf, a pretend teenager with a broken wrist stuck behind an insurance question, a list of patients who'd said no to sharing. <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, rueful, honest.">"Most of the rules I know about,"</span> she said, sitting down, <span class="said" data-who="Maria" data-tone="Quiet, rueful, honest.">"somebody got hurt first. That's how they got written."</span>`,
    `Lucia looked at her for a long moment. <span class="said" data-who="Lucia" data-tone="Soft, wry, a little worried.">"That's kind of a sad essay, Mom."</span> But she started typing.`,
    (s.score>=30
      ?`It had been a hard week that went well. Maria had told the truth to a surveyor on her old unit and tried to put every problem in front of the person who owned it. None of that changed the finding. The finding was about the work before her, and now it was hers.`
      :`It had been a hard week, and some of it she'd want back. Ochoa would go through it on Tuesday, line by line. The finding didn't care which choices were hers. It was hers now either way.`),
    `At 10:15 PM her phone lit up on the counter. Marcus. <span class="said" data-who="Marcus" data-tone="Low, grim, deadpan.">"found why nobody noticed laramie. it's not the interface. you'll want to sit down for this one. tuesday?"</span>`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch02.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`The Plumbing`
});
